# Vercel deploy check

This app deploys on Vercel with `next build`. Before pushing, run that build and ESLint, and do not push until both pass.

```bash
npx eslint . --max-warnings 0
npm run build
```

- `npm run build` is the Vercel build. A type error, a bad import, or two pages resolving to the same URL fails the deployment.
- A green local build does not prove the Vercel build. This app loads fonts with `next/font/google`. Google sometimes returns `fonts.gstatic.com/l/font?kit=…&skey=…` with no `.woff2`. Next.js 16.1.6 then fails on Vercel with `next/font/google queries have exactly one entry` and `Can't resolve '@vercel/turbopack-next/internal/font/google/font'`, traced to a layout such as `app/(studio)/layout.tsx`. Redeploying the same commit does not fix it. Before pushing, `next` and `eslint-config-next` must be at least `16.4.3` (the release that parses those URLs). If they are older, bump both, then run the build again.
- Do not add a second `app/page.tsx` next to `app/(studio)/page.tsx`. Both would claim `/`.
- Keep studio styles under `.studio`. Do not change the global tokens in `app/globals.css` or `tailwind.config.ts` for marketing work.
- Every image path used by the site must exist under `public/`. Do not commit `public/case-studies/unused/` or the base64 prototype HTML.
- Do not commit `.next/`, `.env*`, or `.vercel`.
- If the build fails only because a local env var is missing, say which variable. Do not print its value. Vercel already has the project env.
